00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.980
Because we have really,
this really great footage

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.797
that I just wanted to
show everyone real quick.

00:02:03.797 --> 00:02:04.740
- [Jim Rash] You get ready.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.803
- [Kelly] Here we cut, there we go.

00:02:05.803 --> 00:02:08.112
(everyone cheers and claps loudly)

00:02:08.112 --> 00:02:09.783
- [Jim Rash] Yes. Yes.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.240
Now you probably can't hear the music

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.532
cause you're like,
"what's he dancing to?".

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.270
- I mean, are you dancing
to Proud Mary or is it-

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.850
- No, just silence.
(everyone laughs)

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.200
- [Kelly] that's just si-?
- I love a good silent,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.437
no, that's actually to

00:02:20.437 --> 00:02:23.405
"A Lot of Livin' To Do"
from "Bye Bye Birdie".

00:02:23.405 --> 00:02:24.510
(everyone laughs)

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.520
- We're all shocked Jim
- You got it, right?

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.020
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.430
It's floored everybody in the audience.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.630
- I liked your shimmy.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.100
- No, my friend sent that.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.180
That was early pandemic

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.200
when we thought it was sort of cute,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.070
we were getting puzzles

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.110
and we're just hanging at
our house for like two weeks.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.630
And he sent me the clip
of Anne Margaret dancing.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.460
He says, "I wanna see this recreated".

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.734
So that's what I did in my kitchen.

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:45.581
Cause that's why these
hands are like this.

00:02:45.581 --> 00:02:46.500
(everyone laughs)

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.960
I'm trying my best to do
and that's where her hair

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.610
would be doing fantastic
work and my bald head is not.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.860
- My favorite too is
when people don't realize

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.510
what your tongue is doing.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.050
Like, you know, and your, and your tongue

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.330
is just like, "Mm".
(everyone laughs)

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.200
- [Kelly] You don't realize you're like

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.557
doing something serious and I'm like,

00:02:59.557 --> 00:03:00.630
"why is my tongue hanging out?".

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.774
- Yes. It's basically
a constant neuro face-

00:03:02.774 --> 00:03:04.770
- Pretty great.
- and like that.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.510
- Okay. So Symone, you
were recently part of

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.250
Hillary and Chelsea Clinton's "Gutsy".

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.750
- [Symone] Yes.
- I, I love that whole series.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.116
- [Symone] Thank you
- It was incredible.

00:03:11.116 --> 00:03:11.993
(audience cheers and claps)
- It was amazing, amazing.

00:03:11.993 --> 00:03:14.400
- [Kelly] Yeah, I was so
glad that they came on

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.080
cause I got to watch the whole
thing and it was really cool.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.904
How did that come about? How did that-

00:03:17.904 --> 00:03:20.940
- Hey, look at that.
- [Kelly] Fabulous.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.350
- [Symone] Fabulous darling.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.183
- Wow.

00:03:23.183 --> 00:03:26.040
- Pretty bitch.
(everyone laughs)

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:26.880
- How did it come about?

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.560
Like what, how did you
become a part of It?

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.600
- Oh well they, they sent an email.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.800
You know how all these things go,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.267
And at first my, my manager,
Hunter, he told me and I said,

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.840
"Me? Is this a joke?
You, you playing with me?

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.580
He said, "Yeah", and then of course

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.830
I was honored and floored cause I was

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.210
You do drag, you do all these things.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.470
I've had a lot of great opportunities.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.510
But Hillary Clinton,
wanna come talk to me?

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.694
- [Kelly] In Arkansas.
- [Symone] Yeah.

00:03:46.694 --> 00:03:48.023
- She's from Arkansas.
- And we went back.

00:03:48.023 --> 00:03:49.800
And we, we threw her a party,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.413
at our friend's house back home.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.150
Prom, drag show, party
for Miss Hillary Clinton,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:57.990
who would've thought?

00:03:57.990 --> 00:03:58.823
- You did all of it.
- [Symone] We did all of it.

00:03:58.823 --> 00:03:59.656
- All of it. I love that.

00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:01.050
- [Symone] Yeah, so I got
to perform in front of her.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.850
It was great. It was
a fabulous experience.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.540
- Yeah, the whole series was incredible.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.610
- [Symone] Yeah.
- I love that

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.443
you were highlighted.

00:04:06.443 --> 00:04:07.969
It was really, really cool,
I had to bring that up.

00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:08.850
(audience cheering)
So we mentioned

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.770
how groundbreaking that this movie is,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.167
but what was it like for everyone to work

00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:14.850
with an all LGBTQ+ cast?

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.260
That was cool, like the cast and crew.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.030
- It was a lot of fun. It was like going,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.920
cause you know we have our
own little language with our,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.323
we call each other family.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:21.323 --> 00:04:23.238
- So like it was so much
fun to go and know that

00:04:23.238 --> 00:04:26.610
everyone speaks the same language.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:31.241
It was fun and it just,
it didn't, it felt safe.

00:04:31.241 --> 00:04:32.132
Safe is a good word.

00:04:32.132 --> 00:04:32.965
- I was, I was, I'm so glad you said it.

00:04:32.965 --> 00:04:33.990
I was, "guys, did it feel safe?".

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.180
Like, cause I, I feel like
that when it's people I know,

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.100
like even on "The Voice" and I
go back, we leave for months,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.423
we come back, it's like family.

00:04:39.423 --> 00:04:40.500
- [Symone] Mm-hm
- Like you come back in

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.760
and it's, those are your people.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.440
That, that's gotta be
cool and it's gotta be a

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.320
comfortable environment to be
able to really be creative in?

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.153
- [Billy Eichner] Yeah.
- [Luke Macfarlane] Yeah.

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:50.100
- It also allowed all of us
to make fun of each other.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:50.933
- [Jim Rash] Yeah,
- You know?

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:51.847
(everyone laughs)

00:04:51.847 --> 00:04:53.100
- "Bros"
- Once again love.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.900
- Exactly. "Bros" is a comedy.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.330
You know, it's not like
a super serious thing.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.920
We didn't wanna walk on eggshells

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.390
or be, you know, sanctimonious.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.240
It's like, we want to be
able to spoof each other

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.190
as much as we're spoofing everyone else.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.023
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Because I think

00:05:06.023 --> 00:05:07.440
it's important that we laugh at ourselves

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.430
and we laugh at everyone

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.180
and all of us being in the
community allowed us to do that.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.013
- Yeah.
- Oh my God,

00:05:13.013 --> 00:05:14.640
that was like the scene at the table

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.930
when you're all at the table (laughs).

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.409
- Yes, exactly, yeah. In the boardroom.

00:05:18.409 --> 00:05:20.034
- And you're all sticking
up for your individual,

00:05:20.034 --> 00:05:21.030
(laughs) it was amazing.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.280
So wait, was the plan always to have

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.220
LGBTQ+ actors in the principal roles?

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.690
Was that always from the get go?

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.330
- Pretty early on, I think
the first decision we made

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.720
is that Luke's character,
Aaron, my love interest

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.610
should also be an openly gay man.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.860
And then I turned to Judd
Apatow and Nick Stoller

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.380
who I made the movie with,
and they're both straight men,

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.450
but I said, "you know what? What if

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.390
we're all openly LGBTQ in all the roles?

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.640
Because I think that
will just make the movie

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.980
as funny as it is and
it is mainly a comedy,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.676
but it will add some
meaning to it you know".

00:05:51.676 --> 00:05:52.509
- Authenticity.

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:54.480
- Authenticity was so important

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.270
and luckily everyone went along with it.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.580
And I think it's one of
the most magical things.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.800
You're sitting in a movie
theater and you're laughing

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.510
but then somewhere in
the back of your head,

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.220
you know we're all in the community

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.520
and that we've never gotten
this type of opportunity to just

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.850
fill out the entire cast of a
big studio comedy like this.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.890
Like, you know, we've had "Bridesmaids"

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:14.850
and movies like that,

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:18.000
but we're very rarely a
part of them, you know?

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.920
And we still relate to it and we love them

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.300
and we laugh and we're moved,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.160
but it's nice to see ourselves up there

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.630
and I think it's great for straight people

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.330
to get a real peak at our lives also.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.180
- Yeah, I mean we, we grew up watching

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.120
straight couples fall in love.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.820
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And we laughed with them

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.050
and we cried with them,

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.215
and maybe straight couples can watch

00:06:35.215 --> 00:06:38.790
a gay couple fall in love and
laugh and cry with them too.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:39.623
You know, we certainly have
had lots of experience.

00:06:39.623 --> 00:06:41.430
- [Kelly] Love is love and funny is funny.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.920
Like yeah, I think it's
all, it's all the same.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.530
So I, I wanna ask you
though, speaking of that,

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.810
like growing up, like what
were like, I don't know,

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.709
what was like one of your
favorite romcoms growing up?

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:51.900
- Mm.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.040
- It's hard.
- That's hard.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.480
- It is hard.
- So many.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.430
- [Kelly] I just went through so many.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.400
- I love "Moonstruck".
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.920
- I love Moon Struck.
- [Kelly} Nice.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.480
- I mean growing up, so we're going back.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.209
I love "Working Girl",
"When Harry Met Sally".

00:07:06.209 --> 00:07:07.042
= [Kelly] Yeah.
- You know,

00:07:07.042 --> 00:07:08.220
and we don't get those movies

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.350
in movie theaters anymore , you know.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.910
- [Kelly] We don't really
get them made anymore.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.590
- They don't get made like
"Sleepless in Seattle".

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:14.737
- So thank you for making one (laughs).

00:07:14.737 --> 00:07:16.080
- [Billy Eichner] "You've
Got Mail", exactly.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.000
We just wanted to make
a movie like that cause

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.890
I make a movie that makes you feel good

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.073
when you leave the theater
and hopeful and you know,

00:07:22.073 --> 00:07:25.470
- Usually people don't say
"Moonstruck". Why "Moonstruck"?

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.072
- Well, it's a great movie Kelly Clarkson.

00:07:27.072 --> 00:07:27.905
(everyone laughs)
- It is.

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.647
It is a great movie but in fact,

00:07:29.647 --> 00:07:31.140
it's usually not the one people say.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.550
Why that one for you?

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.590
- It that was a huge movie
when I was growing up

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.625
and I love Cher. Gay.

00:07:36.625 --> 00:07:38.040
- I love Cher.
(everyone laughs)

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.860
- She won the Oscar and it's a great,

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.150
I also grew up in New York.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.500
It's a great New York movie.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.870
It makes New York really magical and-

